# Supabase Security (Physio-Shield)

When editing migrations or Edge Functions, treat security as the default—not an afterthought.

## RLS & Policies

- Every clinical/patient table must have RLS enabled; never leave new tables open to `anon`/`authenticated` without explicit policies.
- Policies must scope by therapist ownership or patient self-access (auth UID linkage)—no broad `USING (true)` on PHI tables.
- Prefer SECURITY DEFINER RPCs only when necessary; pin `search_path` and validate caller role inside the function.
- Grants: least privilege. Do not grant DELETE/UPDATE to roles that only need SELECT/INSERT.

```sql
-- ❌ BAD: mutable search_path, open policy
CREATE FUNCTION public.foo() ... AS $$ ... $$;
CREATE POLICY "open" ON public.patients FOR SELECT USING (true);

-- ✅ GOOD: fixed search_path + ownership check
CREATE FUNCTION public.foo()
RETURNS ...
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = public
AS $$
BEGIN
  -- authorize caller, then act
END;
$$;
```

## Migrations

- Never invent columns/tables outside a migration (AGENTS.md Iron Rule 3).
- New RPCs/triggers touching patients must align with existing audit patterns (`clinical_audit_logs` where applicable).
- After schema changes, update only types/services that match the migrated shape.

## Edge Functions

- Validate JWT / role before acting; do not trust client-supplied patient IDs alone.
- Never log Authorization headers, tokens, or PHI (names, emails, clinical free text).
- Secrets stay in Deno env (`GEMINI_API_KEY`, service role, etc.)—never hardcode.
- Shared helpers under `supabase/functions/_shared/` should scrub or omit identifiers in any diagnostic output.
